# systemd preset policy for the KGSM fleet.
#
# Arch ships `disable *` in 99-default.preset, so without this file every kgsm unit a package
# installs would land disabled and a node would need a person to enable each one by hand. The first
# matching entry across the preset files wins and they are read in lexical order, so 50- sits ahead
# of that default and decides the KGSM units without touching anything else on the host.
#
# A package applies this to its OWN units in post_install and nowhere else: `systemctl preset` on
# first install only, never on upgrade, so an administrator who disabled something keeps it disabled
# across every later version.

# ── The engine's resident half and the leaves that need no credential ─────────
enable kgsm-watchdog.service
enable kgsm-monitor.service
enable kgsm-scheduler.service
enable kgsm-reactor.service

# The per-server network meter. It is enabled rather than opt-in because it rides the watchdog: the
# unit is ordered after kgsm-watchdog and wanted by it, so it attaches to kgsm.slice once that slice
# exists and re-attaches on every later watchdog start. On a host without the watchdog it simply
# never runs, and kgsm-monitor reports rxBps/txBps as null — measured absence, never a fabricated 0.
enable kgsm-net-meter.service

# Journal retention. A system timer running as the kgsm account, so one enable covers a node with no
# login session — which `systemctl --user` cannot, since it acts on one user's session and a package
# scriptlet has no user to act for.
enable kgsm-journal-prune.timer

# ── The leaves a person has to give credentials to ────────────────────────────
# Enabled here so they come up on the next boot once configured; the node's post-transaction hook
# still refuses to START one whose env file holds keys nobody has set.
enable kgsm-api.service
enable kgsm-bot.service
enable kgsm-assistant-service.service

# ── Socket-activated: the socket is the unit that is enabled ──────────────────
# Enabling the .service as well would defeat the activation — kgsm-firewall's daemon holds root and
# kgsm-speech's holds ~1.6GB of models, and both are meant to exit when nobody is asking. An
# inactive .service is each one working correctly.
enable kgsm-firewall.socket
enable kgsm-speech.socket
disable kgsm-speech.service
# kgsm-firewall.service carries no [Install] section — it is static, and a preset line for a static
# unit changes nothing.

# ── Opt-in: installed, and a decision rather than a default ───────────────────
# The indexer starts building a retrieval corpus, which costs GPU time and disk on a host that may
# have wanted the assistant alone. The assistant answers without it.
disable kgsm-rag-indexer.service

# The llama.cpp inference backend. Ollama is the alternative and a host runs one of them, so neither
# can be a default: enabling these on a host serving models through Ollama would bind their ports
# and load a second copy of the weights. `kgsm-llama-use-backend` moves a host between the two.
# kgsm-llama-chat-proxy.service is static — socket-activated by kgsm-llama-chat.socket — so it takes
# no line here.
disable kgsm-llama-chat.socket
disable kgsm-llama-chat.service
disable kgsm-llama-embed.service
